      "name": null,
      "problem": "The cheetah is considered the fastest land animal. It can achieve a speed of $120$ kilometers per hour. What is this speed in kilometers per minute?",
      "worked_solution": "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{h}$ with $\\text{min}.$ To get rid of $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{h}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{60}$ hours per minute.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =(120\\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cheetah can run at a speed $2\\,\\text{km}/\\text{min}.$",
